    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "Issue #26848 states that things start breaking down around a scale of 100_000 for a feature, according to the above simulations.",
      "PR #33020 discussion says the PR removes the need to upcast float32 inputs to float64 because the eigen GCV path is now numerically stable enough to pass the usual tests.",
      "PR #33020 body notes that float32 inputs were parametrized in LOO equivalence tests, with a note to re-add the float32 to float64 upcast if results are bad.",
      "The RidgeGCV fit code comment says float32 can be numerically unstable for this estimator, so inputs are converted to float64 when the array API namespace and device allow.",
      "The removed array API test previously set rtol=1e-3 for RidgeGCV with float32 only when the device/namespace could not use float64 (torch with mps); this is a specific case, not a separation tolerance.",
      "No passage states a precision value (e.g. ULP count or error bound) for the discrepancy, and no passage states the tolerance at which correct and incorrect answers separate; the default-tolerance claim in the question is not supported by the supplied passages.",
      "The buggy-output and expected-output values (score=-37754.60 vs 1.00, coefficients -9221.16/11981.91 vs -0.00/94.78) come from the numerical failure record, not from the passages on float32 or magnitude."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "The breakdown magnitude of 100_000 comes from issue text referring to simulations that are not supplied, so the exact conditions are unknown.",
      "Whether the discrepancy appears in float32 depends on whether the upcast to float64 is active, which varies by namespace and device.",
      "The removed rtol=1e-3 applied only to a specific RidgeGCV / torch-mps float32 case and is not a general separation tolerance.",
      "The magnitude regime (100_000) in #26848 is a different issue from the reported failure in #32459 and may not match the small-alpha trigger conditions.",
